261
00:17:55,560 --> 00:17:59,770
Right, then come back down.

262
00:17:59,770 --> 00:18:02,370
That works.

263
00:18:02,370 --> 00:18:04,380
Here, like this.

264
00:18:04,380 --> 00:18:08,480
Or instead of lying back, you fall face
down.

265
00:18:08,480 --> 00:18:10,410
Just—bam—face down.

266
00:18:10,410 --> 00:18:13,190
You fall, and the camera moves up.

267
00:18:13,190 --> 00:18:14,910
Then you're already lying on top of him.

268
00:18:14,910 --> 00:18:15,910
Okay.

269
00:18:15,910 --> 00:18:18,280
Shall we do it again?

270
00:18:18,280 --> 00:18:26,030
Let's... [partly unclear]

271
00:18:26,030 --> 00:18:27,930
Okay. Let's do it again.

272
00:18:27,930 --> 00:18:31,730
That was good.

273
00:18:31,920 --> 00:18:37,600
03 The audience is inside August 26, 2023
· Ciyu Bookstore

274
00:19:01,380 --> 00:19:06,320
Please switch to silent mode.

275
00:19:06,320 --> 00:19:09,390
Our performance is about to begin.

276
00:19:09,390 --> 00:19:15,150
Enjoy the evening.

277
00:20:37,920 --> 00:20:42,600
After the performance A discussion of
theater, film and documentation

278
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:46,263
The point is to

279
00:20:46,263 --> 00:20:52,790
bring the camera into theater.

280
00:20:52,790 --> 00:20:54,890
That's what I've been trying to do.

281
00:20:54,890 --> 00:20:59,550
I think this production worked quite well.

282
00:20:59,550 --> 00:21:04,140
I'll keep going further along this path.

283
00:21:04,140 --> 00:21:08,430
So, in a sense, the era of P4 Theater has
ended.

284
00:21:08,430 --> 00:21:12,460
Making a theater production is no longer
the final goal.

285
00:21:12,460 --> 00:21:13,948
Theater,

286
00:21:13,948 --> 00:21:20,830
film and documentation come together.

287
00:21:20,830 --> 00:21:24,870
There is a record of filming and creating
together,

288
00:21:24,870 --> 00:21:29,660
and a finished film to show to others.

289
00:21:29,660 --> 00:21:33,720
The live theater is still there too. It
all exists together.

290
00:21:33,730 --> 00:21:37,617
This documentation may be

291
00:21:37,617 --> 00:21:41,660
more than a simple record.

292
00:21:41,660 --> 00:21:46,200
I'm still trying to find a way to describe
it.

293
00:21:46,200 --> 00:21:49,020
It isn't a documentary, nor is it that
kind of...

294
00:21:49,020 --> 00:21:53,320
It isn't just a record of the creative
process.

295
00:21:53,320 --> 00:21:59,980
The audience was there, and they were
filmed too.

296
00:21:59,980 --> 00:22:04,170
Their reactions, their looks — all of that
is in it.

297
00:22:04,170 --> 00:22:12,003
It captures something immediate and real,

298
00:22:12,003 --> 00:22:16,970
beyond the process itself.

299
00:22:16,970 --> 00:22:21,236
So I feel this record contains

300
00:22:21,236 --> 00:22:26,070
something beyond theater and film.

301
00:22:26,070 --> 00:22:26,700
Right.

302
00:22:26,700 --> 00:22:33,970
Perhaps we haven't found a more precise
word for this kind of record.

303
00:22:33,970 --> 00:22:36,080
But it certainly isn't a documentary.

304
00:22:36,240 --> 00:22:41,920
04 From shooting to editing Editing,
screenings and sound

305
00:22:42,310 --> 00:22:46,320
Making my film might look easy.

306
00:22:46,320 --> 00:22:49,340
I put an enormous amount of work into it.

307
00:22:49,340 --> 00:22:53,340
Just learning to use the camera took me
ages.

308
00:22:54,470 --> 00:22:56,480
It's all technical stuff.

309
00:22:56,480 --> 00:23:02,660
Now it's editing — how to cut it on the
computer,

310
00:23:02,660 --> 00:23:05,390
how to do the color grading...

311
00:23:05,390 --> 00:23:09,460
[Unclear] I'm learning every day.

312
00:23:09,460 --> 00:23:13,830
It gives me a headache. I edit every day.

313
00:23:13,830 --> 00:23:16,110
I've done almost everything myself.

314
00:23:16,110 --> 00:23:18,750
Never mind the shoot. Just in
post-production,

315
00:23:18,750 --> 00:23:23,600
editing, color grading, subtitles,
compositing,

316
00:23:23,600 --> 00:23:27,510
adding sound effects — I've done it all.

317
00:23:27,510 --> 00:23:30,550
Really.

318
00:24:30,850 --> 00:24:31,652
Fuck,

319
00:24:31,652 --> 00:24:38,550
some of the audio tracks are missing.
Damn.

320
00:24:38,550 --> 00:24:43,570
I'd like to change the opening music and
use that one at the end.

321
00:24:43,570 --> 00:24:45,770
I mean, don't make the beginning and
ending the same.

322
00:24:45,770 --> 00:24:47,560
They could be the same.

323
00:24:47,560 --> 00:24:52,340
Shouldn't there be a difference? Otherwise
it's just reversed.

324
00:24:52,340 --> 00:24:55,550
No, I mean, if we use that at the
beginning...

325
00:24:57,930 --> 00:25:02,190
Okay, but I want that revolving effect
too.

326
00:25:02,190 --> 00:25:06,320
Yours revolves at the start; mine does
too. There's a loop.

327
00:25:06,320 --> 00:25:08,280
I'll have a look. Help me think it
through.

328
00:25:08,280 --> 00:25:14,750
If we use that at the beginning... I used
it twice, exactly the same.

329
00:25:14,750 --> 00:25:19,551
Help me change it. Make a new one,

330
00:25:19,551 --> 00:25:24,210
so the opening and ending differ.

331
00:25:24,210 --> 00:25:28,260
Okay. Making them the same is too silly.

332
00:25:28,260 --> 00:25:34,857
It's exhausting to watch.

333
00:25:34,857 --> 00:25:41,190
Really tiring, isn't it?

334
00:25:41,190 --> 00:25:46,190
[Unclear] It could go to Cannes...

335
00:25:46,190 --> 00:25:49,359
Yes, I felt it too. Everyone did, you too.

336
00:25:49,359 --> 00:25:54,189
But the dizziness in the second and third
sections is different.

337
00:25:54,189 --> 00:25:58,566
The third is the rehearsal footage I shot
— very physical.

338
00:25:58,566 --> 00:26:02,490
It's mainly the earlier overlays that feel
dizzying.

339
00:26:02,490 --> 00:26:09,220
In the second section, [unclear]. A lot of
the overlays work well.

340
00:26:09,220 --> 00:26:13,000
But I need to change the second section.

341
00:26:13,000 --> 00:26:20,000
One moving image and one still image —
that feels comfortable to me.

342
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:22,000
Okay, I'll try that.

343
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:27,000
Sometimes it feels like there are three
images at once, all moving.

344
00:26:27,000 --> 00:26:29,800
Then it's too much, right? It gets a
little dizzying.

345
00:26:29,800 --> 00:26:31,000
Okay, I'll change it.

346
00:26:31,000 --> 00:26:36,600
Movement in the upper image is okay. But
if the shot is too... [unclear]

347
00:26:36,600 --> 00:26:38,760
Let's go downstairs and talk over food.

348
00:26:38,760 --> 00:26:45,140
This should be your final version. The
completely final version.

349
00:26:45,140 --> 00:26:48,212
But there's another issue:

350
00:26:48,212 --> 00:26:54,120
your sound needs to inspire me, and then I
revise.

351
00:26:54,120 --> 00:26:59,930
I need that exchange with you. Your sound
has to give me something new.

352
00:26:59,930 --> 00:27:02,990
Then how do we keep it in sync?

353
00:27:02,990 --> 00:27:07,080
I think we still need to discuss things
together.

354
00:27:07,080 --> 00:27:10,670
But how do you line it up?

355
00:27:10,670 --> 00:27:13,892
When you give me the sound,

356
00:27:13,892 --> 00:27:19,620
I can rework parts of the edit to my own
rhythm.

357
00:27:19,620 --> 00:27:26,780
But when you change sections or the
structure, that can't be altered.

358
00:27:26,780 --> 00:27:29,200
It can be changed.

359
00:27:29,200 --> 00:27:35,467
If you change the rhythm, how do you
revise it?

360
00:27:35,467 --> 00:27:40,000
You have to work with the sound...

361
00:27:40,000 --> 00:27:48,810
Not just the duration... [unclear]

362
00:27:48,810 --> 00:27:50,610
No, let me explain how I work.

363
00:27:50,610 --> 00:27:53,370
Say you give me five seconds of action.

364
00:27:53,370 --> 00:27:59,400
But to me, that isn't five seconds. It's a
number of musical bars.

365
00:27:59,400 --> 00:28:02,790
That's so complicated. Can you work in
that much detail?

366
00:28:02,790 --> 00:28:06,080
What else can I do? It's the only way.

367
00:28:06,080 --> 00:28:09,580
Work in bars. — Of course I work in bars.

368
00:28:09,580 --> 00:28:13,680
Over two hours, all in bars? — You don't
give me all of it.

369
00:28:13,680 --> 00:28:19,660
How many seconds are there in one bar?

370
00:28:19,660 --> 00:28:22,137
It depends on the tempo.

371
00:28:22,137 --> 00:28:28,020
The way I work is different from the way
an editor works.

372
00:28:28,020 --> 00:28:31,600
I don't have seconds here. I have bars.

373
00:28:31,600 --> 00:28:41,054
If I send it back and you change it again,

374
00:28:41,054 --> 00:28:44,880
it won't line up.

375
00:28:44,880 --> 00:28:48,961
It doesn't go back again.

376
00:28:48,961 --> 00:28:58,430
By the time it reaches me, it's basically
the final stage.

377
00:28:58,430 --> 00:29:04,693
The overall structure is what we have
today.

378
00:29:04,693 --> 00:29:06,970
It won't change.

379
00:29:06,970 --> 00:29:15,778
Then cut the loop yourself. — You don't
know how?

380
00:29:15,778 --> 00:29:21,710
— How do I cut it? — Just cut it.

381
00:29:21,710 --> 00:29:26,132
I think we need a way

382
00:29:26,132 --> 00:29:32,450
to do it as you're suggesting.

383
00:29:32,450 --> 00:29:38,590
First you send me a section of a given
length,

384
00:29:39,980 --> 00:29:43,770
I send back five minutes, and you revise
it again, right?

385
00:29:43,770 --> 00:29:46,070
Then don't change those five minutes.

386
00:29:46,070 --> 00:29:49,040
Right. That works.

387
00:29:49,040 --> 00:29:52,700
You can only change what's inside. The
start and end are fixed.

388
00:29:52,700 --> 00:29:55,320
You can't turn it into five minutes and
one second.

389
00:29:55,320 --> 00:29:58,080
Right. That definitely works.

390
00:29:58,080 --> 00:30:02,140
So the duration stays the same, but the
content can change.

391
00:30:02,140 --> 00:30:07,440
Yes, change the content, but not the
duration. If that changes, it won't work.

392
00:30:07,600 --> 00:30:13,280
05 Who is the film for? Returning to the
post-performance discussion

393
00:30:13,650 --> 00:30:16,670
So we still need to have a camera.

394
00:30:16,670 --> 00:30:20,672
When you put it together into a story,

395
00:30:20,672 --> 00:30:26,360
something for others to watch, what holds
it together?

396
00:30:26,360 --> 00:30:28,140
People need to be able to understand it.

397
00:30:28,140 --> 00:30:33,308
If nobody understands it in the end,

398
00:30:33,308 --> 00:30:36,610
that won't work either.

399
00:30:36,610 --> 00:30:41,107
Understanding it seems important,

400
00:30:41,107 --> 00:30:45,740
but maybe it isn't that important.

401
00:30:45,740 --> 00:30:52,030
Take a simple example: A Chinese Odyssey.

402
00:30:52,030 --> 00:30:56,370
When it came out in '98, it flopped.

403
00:30:56,370 --> 00:31:03,770
Ten years later, people on the Shuimu
Tsinghua BBS brought it back.

404
00:31:03,770 --> 00:31:06,191
Suddenly everyone seemed to understand it.

405
00:31:06,191 --> 00:31:08,900
People's readings keep changing with the
times.

406
00:31:08,900 --> 00:31:14,280
But it has a main storyline. People didn't
like that storyline at first.

407
00:31:14,280 --> 00:31:19,530
Only later did it become a storyline
people liked.

408
00:31:19,530 --> 00:31:22,990
So understanding can come with a delay.

409
00:31:22,990 --> 00:31:27,807
Put another way, it's about a storyline.

410
00:31:27,807 --> 00:31:31,300
You need a central narrative.

411
00:31:31,300 --> 00:31:36,240
Something good... What I mean by something
good is...

412
00:31:36,240 --> 00:31:41,950
It can be understood in different ways — a
thousand ways.

413
00:31:41,950 --> 00:31:46,060
Each time I watch it, I find something new
in what I already know.

414
00:31:46,060 --> 00:31:49,940
So it doesn't matter. You can always
understand something.

415
00:31:49,940 --> 00:31:53,870
It's like your own life.

416
00:31:53,870 --> 00:32:00,860
You always think you understand, but you
can never understand it completely.

417
00:32:00,860 --> 00:32:04,430
If you really understood it all, what
would be left to live for?

418
00:32:04,640 --> 00:32:09,320
